WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:02.936
You need to tell your agent about your project,

2
00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.939
the mission, audience, and other decisions.

3
00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:08.641
In this lesson, we'll work together

4
00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:11.144
with the agent to write the Constitution.

5
00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:13.613
What really is your project?

6
00:00:14.114 --> 00:00:17.117
Say you're working on a new web app for your company.

7
00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:19.819
What's the core idea behind its development?

8
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:23.423
How does it fit within your company's preferred tech stack?

9
00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:25.625
What features are planned?

10
00:00:25.825 --> 00:00:28.828
These three foundational principles form the Constitution,

11
00:00:28.828 --> 00:00:31.631
a global set of high-level requirements

12
00:00:31.631 --> 00:00:34.634
that will guide future feature development

13
00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:37.804
and explain the project's shape to stakeholders.

14
00:00:38.104 --> 00:00:41.908
For example, you have so many choices for your tech stack.

15
00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:46.613
You'll want to narrow down your options based on appropriate tradeoffs

16
00:00:46.813 --> 00:00:48.915
and what you use at your company.

17
00:00:49.282 --> 00:00:51.384
We need to write this down.

18
00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:55.088
for the agent, for your teammates, for the future.

19
00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:57.290
But we don't write it alone.

20
00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:01.094
We write it in a conversation with the agent.

21
00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:04.798
You'll be surprised at the great questions it will ask.

22
00:01:05.165 --> 00:01:07.767
architecture patterns you hadn't considered,

23
00:01:08.068 --> 00:01:10.970
external packages that already do the work,

24
00:01:11.371 --> 00:01:13.239
or tradeoffs. For example,

25
00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.542
speed versus data fidelity.

26
00:01:15.942 --> 00:01:19.946
The mission, tech stack, and roadmap aren't just properties

27
00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:22.649
of a Greenfield project you're starting from scratch.

28
00:01:22.949 --> 00:01:25.552
Existing codebases use these three pillars too.

29
00:01:26.152 --> 00:01:29.956
We will talk about bringing the SDD workflow to existing projects

30
00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:31.958
in just a few videos from now.

31
00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:36.763
Here's the example project
we'll use throughout this course.

32
00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:40.133
We are writing AgentClinic, a place for AI agents

33
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:42.535
to get relief from their humans.

34
00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:47.974
AgentClinic is a fun parody of the popular learning project Pet Clinic.

35
00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:50.477
Coding agents are doing a lot of work.

36
00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:53.146
It's stressful. Hallucinations,

37
00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.548
context rot, memory issues and

38
00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:58.151
co-worker sub-agent coordination

39
00:01:58.651 --> 00:02:00.653
all take a toll on agent health.

40
00:02:01.154 --> 00:02:04.457
Let's build a clinic where they can get help with their issues.

41
00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:08.528
It's a full stack web app with a Next.js back end

42
00:02:08.828 --> 00:02:10.530
and a React front end.

43
00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.032
The app allows you to manage appointments,

44
00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:15.034
ailments like hallucination,

45
00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:17.537
and treatments like a context infusion.

46
00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:21.307
Let's take a look at how you might go about writing

47
00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:24.077
a detailed spec for a project like this.

48
00:02:25.044 --> 00:02:27.080
Let's chat with the agent.

49
00:02:27.914 --> 00:02:30.917
First, we provide our project description to the agent

50
00:02:31.317 --> 00:02:34.120
and tell it that our stakeholders gave their input

51
00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:37.924
in README.md, which we added to the repo.

52
00:02:38.158 --> 00:02:40.960
In the important note, we directly mention

53
00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.963
Claude code's AskUserQuestion tool.

54
00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.666
This tool is totally optional, but

55
00:02:46.866 --> 00:02:49.068
we just like the way it looks in the interface.

56
00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:51.905
Also, for the project's constitution,

57
00:02:51.905 --> 00:02:55.208
tell the agent to work with you on a mission,

58
00:02:55.208 --> 00:02:57.911
tech stack, and roadmap.

59
00:02:58.211 --> 00:03:00.213
Spec-driven development works best

60
00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:02.215
with a human-in-the-loop approach

61
00:03:02.215 --> 00:03:04.417
where you review small changes.

62
00:03:04.918 --> 00:03:07.420
So tell the agent to organize the roadmap

63
00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:09.222
in small steps.

64
00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:11.724
As we work together, you'll see

65
00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:14.027
the agent ask some really good questions.

66
00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:16.629
You might see different ones.

67
00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:21.267
The first question says, what tone should the mission.md take?

68
00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:24.737
I'll choose, you guessed it, playful.

69
00:03:25.438 --> 00:03:29.142
That covers the mission. Now some questions for the tech stack.

70
00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:32.845
Our engineers are used to TypeScript on the back end,

71
00:03:33.246 --> 00:03:35.448
so let's add that to the requirements.

72
00:03:36.449 --> 00:03:38.151
Next for our roadmap.

73
00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:41.287
It asks how granular it should be.

74
00:03:41.287 --> 00:03:43.289
I'll choose the first option.

75
00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.292
We finished the initial interview.

76
00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.294
Let's choose submit answers.

77
00:03:49.095 --> 00:03:51.497
The agent will ask you for write permissions.

78
00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:54.300
This keeps changes under your control.

79
00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:57.704
If you are comfortable with the security tradeoffs,

80
00:03:58.104 --> 00:04:01.007
you can select the option to approve all instances

81
00:04:01.107 --> 00:04:03.710
of a particular command for this session.

82
00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.112
You'll see this in the rest of the course.

83
00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:14.721
Now we're done and we have three files in the specs directory.

84
00:04:15.121 --> 00:04:19.325
mission.md, tech-stack.md, roadmap.md.

85
00:04:20.026 --> 00:04:22.829
It's human in the loop time. Let's review.

86
00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:29.636
For example, the mission left out a target audience.

87
00:04:29.869 --> 00:04:33.373
That's reasonable. How could the agent know your business?

88
00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:37.377
Instead of editing directly, let's continue the conversation.

89
00:04:38.778 --> 00:04:40.880
To keep all artifacts consistent,

90
00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:42.882
it is better practice to ask

91
00:04:42.882 --> 00:04:44.684
the agent to make changes to them.

92
00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:48.488
Manually, and you might miss updating related documents.

93
00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.059
We want to use SQLite for this project

94
00:04:53.059 --> 00:04:55.261
since this is a quick prototype.

95
00:04:55.461 --> 00:04:59.465
We didn't mention it, but the agent figured it out in recommendations.

96
00:04:59.766 --> 00:05:04.470
It's now part of our tech stack. Do one final review.

97
00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:09.971
It's important to get everything right up front. Let's commit the Constitution

98
00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:13.880
so it will be a living document for the project.

99
00:05:15.281 --> 00:05:17.583
AgentClinic now has a constitution,

100
00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:21.287
mission, roadmap, tech stack, to guide our project.

101
00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.489
We're ready to tackle our first feature.
